\begin{frame}

\begin{itemize}
\item Why is this test called an ``exact'' test?
\item Because we know the \textit{exact} distribution of our estimate under the specified null. We do not have to approximate the distribution.
% \item This will not be true for all of our hypothesis testing\dots
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
% \begin{frame}{Hypotheses}
%
% \begin{itemize}
% \item We framed our null hypothesis as below:
%
% $$
% H_0: \tau_i = 0, \text{ for all } i \text{ in our pop.}
% $$
%
%
% \item Implicitly, the alternative is that for some individual(s), the treatment effect is non-zero.
%
% $$
% H_A: \tau_i \neq 0, \text{ for some } i \text{ in our pop.}
% $$
%
%
%
% \item In our case, we did not find strong evidence to reject the null hypothesis, i.e., our data is consistent with what we would see if the null hypothesis were true.
%
%
%
% \item Note that we do NOT say that we reject or accept the alternative hypothesis.
%
%
%
% \item We can only say that our results were not consistent with or were consistent with what we would have seen under the null--i.e., we have evidence to reject or fail to reject the null.
% \end{itemize}
%
%
% \end{frame}
%
%
% %%%%%NOTE%%%%%
% \note{
% \scriptsize \singlespacing
%
% \begin{itemize}
% \item xxxx
% \end{itemize}
% \\~\
% }
%
% %-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
% \begin{frame}{Distributions of estimators}
%
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Our difference in means estimator is a function of the data we observe.
% \item Because there is randomness in the data, here, due to random assignment of treatment, the estimator is also a random variable.
% \item Just like other random variables have distributions to describe them, estimators also have distributions.
% \item We don't know the \textit{true} distribution of the difference in means estimator, for the same reason that we don't know individual treatment effects.\textcolor{red}{(FPoCI!)}
% \item But we DO know what the distribution would be under the null.
% \end{itemize}
%
% \end{frame}
%
%
% %%%%%NOTE%%%%%
% \note{
% \scriptsize \singlespacing
%
% \begin{itemize}
% \item xxxx
% \end{itemize}
% \\~\
% }
%
% %-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}

Note that we are conducting inference with respect to:

\begin{itemize}
\item a defined population
\item a defined treatment
\item a defined outcome
\item a known treatment assignment mechanism
\item a specified estimand
\item a specified estimating procedure
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}{}

\begin{itemize}
\item We could have used a different estimand
\item Or a different estimator
\item Randomization inference is also helpful because we can account for the nature of the treatment assignment procedure
\begin{itemize}
\item For example, if treatment is assigned by clusters
\item Or matched pairs
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{The null}

\begin{itemize}
\item How do we determine what the null is?
\item We formalize our hypotheses in terms of the effect we are trying to find in the data. Is there a treatment effect? Is there a difference between these two groups?
\item The null is (often, but not always) the case when there is no effect, or no difference.
\item We can imagine other kinds of hypotheses, for example that effects are bounded away from zero and positive, or exactly .2. And we can characterize the distribution of our test statistic under the null.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{The null}

\begin{itemize}
\item Is the sharp null of no individual level effects plausible in this setting?
\item Does it matter?
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


